+"""GPU-first memory-reduced copy of the upstream SongBench eval.py.
+
+This file is a copy of the upstream `eval.py` (unmodified original is kept
+byte-for-byte in the same directory).  Only the changes needed to make the
+evaluator fit an 8 GB laptop GPU are added; the scoring math is untouched.
+
+Reward-model config / checkpoint are still read from the relative paths
+`configs/songbench.yaml` and `ckpt/songbench.safetensors`, so **cwd must be
+songbench-toolkit/** (same as the upstream script).
+
+Memory ladder (--mem_level):
+  1  PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True (set before CUDA init)
+     + torch.cuda.empty_cache() between tracks.  fp32, unchanged numerics.
+  2  + do not materialise the 25-entry hidden_states tuple of MuQ: the reward
+     model only consumes hidden_states[6], which is the output of
+     conformer.layers[5].  We run preprocessing + conv + layers[0..5] and keep
+     that single tensor (identical object the original tuple held).
+  3  + cast MuQ and the reward model to bfloat16 (input too).  This changes
+     numbers; an equivalence check against the fp32 path is mandatory.
+  4  + run MuQ's acoustic front-end (preprocessing -> normalize -> conv) on the
+     CPU and only the conformer + reward model on the GPU.  Measured
+     2026-10-05: the front-end is what OOMs on long tracks (the 2048-bin
+     melspec and the Res2d maps, ~2.48 GB for a 406 s track); the conformer
+     input is only [1, T', 1024] (~62 MB at 406 s), so keeping the front-end off
+     the GPU removes the peak without touching the sequence the conformer sees.
+     Device placement only -> numerics unchanged up to CPU/GPU kernel rounding;
+     an equivalence check against level 1 is still required.
+     NOTE: level 4 is fp32.  The bfloat16 cast belongs to level 3 alone
+     (`mem_level == 3`): MuQ's preprocessing forces the melspec to float16
+     whenever the input is 16-bit, which then collides with bfloat16 conv
+     weights ("Input type (c10::Half) and bias type (c10::BFloat16)").  Level 4
+     must therefore not inherit level 3's dtype.
+
+  Levels 1/2/3/4 are separate configurations, not a strictly additive stack:
+  pick the lowest one that fits, and keep one level for a whole scoring set so
+  every number in a comparison comes from the same evaluator config.
+
+Orthogonal, numerically-safe patches (--lowmem True):
+  a) MuQ conformer self-attention -> F.scaled_dot_product_attention.  The
+     upstream code materialises scores (B,16,T,T) and softmax probs (another
+     B,16,T,T); at T~10150 that is ~6.6 GB for one tensor.  SDPA is the same
+     math without the T^2 materialisation (scale = 1/sqrt(head_size), same as
+     upstream).
+  b) MuQ conv front-end: in-place ReLU / additive residual so the residual
+     module keeps 2 instead of 3 live feature maps (each 512x64x(T/2) fp32,
+     2.6 GB at 406 s).
+  c) reward model: set each nn.MultiheadAttention.dropout to 0.0.  In eval
+     mode dropout is already a no-op, but the non-zero value disables PyTorch's
+     fused (memory-efficient) attention fast path, which then materialises
+     (B*H,T,T) again.  Setting it to 0.0 is behaviourally identical in eval and
+     re-enables the fused path.
